{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "qnxport",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "semanticVersion": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/nyxlabs29-cyber/qnxport",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "QNX001",
              "name": "EpollEventLoop",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "epoll event loop"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "epoll has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference. The ionotify() page states that it 'corresponds to the Linux epoll() function and BSD's kqueue()'."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Use poll()/ppoll()/select() when you only wait on file descriptors. Use ionotify() with a SIGEV_PULSE event (registered with MsgRegisterEvent()) and receive it with MsgReceivePulse() when the loop must also serve QNX messages, timers or pulses from other threads. ionotify() arms are one-shot: re-arm after every delivery. (confidence: documented)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Use poll()/ppoll()/select() when you only wait on file descriptors. Use ionotify() with a SIGEV_PULSE event (registered with MsgRegisterEvent()) and receive it with MsgReceivePulse() when the loop must also serve QNX messages, timers or pulses from other threads. ionotify() arms are one-shot: re-arm after every delivery.\n\n**Why:** epoll has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference. The ionotify() page states that it 'corresponds to the Linux epoll() function and BSD's kqueue()'.\n\n- [ionotify()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/ionotify.html)\n- [poll(), ppoll()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/poll.html)\n- [select(), pselect()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/s/select.html)\n- [MsgReceivePulse()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/m/msgreceivepulse.html)\n- [MsgRegisterEvent()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/m/msgregisterevent.html)\n- [sigevent](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/s/sigevent.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/ionotify.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "events",
                  "confidence-documented"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "documented"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX005",
              "name": "InotifyFilesystemWatches",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "inotify filesystem watches"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 documents inotify (libfsnotify), but it is served by the fsevmgr filesystem event manager: the program must link -lfsnotify, /dev/fsnotify must exist and be readable, and inotify_init() fails if fsevmgr is not running."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Keep the inotify code, add -lfsnotify to the link line, make sure fsevmgr runs on the target, and handle inotify_init() failure at start-up. Check event coverage per filesystem on your target (verify). inotify_qnx_ext() enables QNX-specific extensions. (confidence: documented)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Keep the inotify code, add -lfsnotify to the link line, make sure fsevmgr runs on the target, and handle inotify_init() failure at start-up. Check event coverage per filesystem on your target (verify). inotify_qnx_ext() enables QNX-specific extensions.\n\n**Why:** QNX 8.0 documents inotify (libfsnotify), but it is served by the fsevmgr filesystem event manager: the program must link -lfsnotify, /dev/fsnotify must exist and be readable, and inotify_init() fails if fsevmgr is not running.\n\n- [inotify_init(), inotify_init1()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/inotify_init.html)\n- [inotify_add_watch()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/inotify_add_watch.html)\n- [fsevmgr](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.utilities/topic/f/fsevmgr.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/inotify_init.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "events",
                  "confidence-documented"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "documented"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX007",
              "name": "Prctl",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "prctl()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "prctl has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: PR_SET_NAME / PR_GET_NAME map to pthread_setname_np(0, name) / pthread_getname_np() (documented; the length limit is _NTO_THREAD_NAME_MAX instead of Linux's 16 bytes). Other options (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, PR_SET_DUMPABLE, PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, PR_SET_SECCOMP ...) have no documented single-call equivalent: verify, or use procmgr_ability() for privilege control. (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** PR_SET_NAME / PR_GET_NAME map to pthread_setname_np(0, name) / pthread_getname_np() (documented; the length limit is _NTO_THREAD_NAME_MAX instead of Linux's 16 bytes). Other options (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, PR_SET_DUMPABLE, PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, PR_SET_SECCOMP ...) have no documented single-call equivalent: verify, or use procmgr_ability() for privilege control.\n\n**Why:** prctl has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference.\n\n- [pthread_setname_np()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/pthread_setname_np.html)\n- [pthread_getname_np()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/pthread_getname_np.html)\n- [procmgr_ability()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/procmgr_ability.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/pthread_setname_np.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "process",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX008",
              "name": "CpuAffinity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CPU affinity"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "sched_setaffinity / pthread_setaffinity_np and the CPU_* macros have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: For the calling thread use ThreadCtl(_NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK, &mask) (also _NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK_GET_AND_SET and _NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK_GET_AND_SET_INHERIT); map CPU numbers to bits of the runmask. The ThreadCtl() page says the runmask commands can only be executed on the calling thread, so code that sets the affinity of ANOTHER thread or process (sched_setaffinity(pid, ...)) has no documented equivalent: the target thread must set its own runmask (verify). sched_getcpu() has no documented equivalent: verify. (confidence: documented)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** For the calling thread use ThreadCtl(_NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK, &mask) (also _NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK_GET_AND_SET and _NTO_TCTL_RUNMASK_GET_AND_SET_INHERIT); map CPU numbers to bits of the runmask. The ThreadCtl() page says the runmask commands can only be executed on the calling thread, so code that sets the affinity of ANOTHER thread or process (sched_setaffinity(pid, ...)) has no documented equivalent: the target thread must set its own runmask (verify). sched_getcpu() has no documented equivalent: verify.\n\n**Why:** sched_setaffinity / pthread_setaffinity_np and the CPU_* macros have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference.\n\n- [ThreadCtl()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/t/threadctl.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/t/threadctl.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "process",
                  "confidence-documented"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "documented"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX009",
              "name": "ZeroCopyTransferSendfileSpliceTee",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "zero-copy transfer (sendfile/splice/tee)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "sendfile, splice, tee and vmsplice have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Fall back to a read()/write() loop (or mmap() of the source file). Keep the Linux path behind an #ifdef and add a portable copy loop. Check your SDP for any BSD-style sendfile() before relying on it (verify). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Fall back to a read()/write() loop (or mmap() of the source file). Keep the Linux path behind an #ifdef and add a portable copy loop. Check your SDP for any BSD-style sendfile() before relying on it (verify).\n\n**Why:** sendfile, splice, tee and vmsplice have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference.\n\n- [read()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/r/read.html)\n- [mmap()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/m/mmap.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/r/read.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "io",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX012",
              "name": "SysinfoLoadCpuCountHelpers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "sysinfo / load / CPU count helpers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "sys/sysinfo.h, sysinfo(), get_nprocs() and getloadavg() have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: sysconf() is documented for configurable limits (page size, open files, ...). For CPU count, memory totals and uptime check which sysconf names your SDP defines and the system page (<sys/syspage.h>); there is no documented load-average call (verify). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** sysconf() is documented for configurable limits (page size, open files, ...). For CPU count, memory totals and uptime check which sysconf names your SDP defines and the system page (<sys/syspage.h>); there is no documented load-average call (verify).\n\n**Why:** sys/sysinfo.h, sysinfo(), get_nprocs() and getloadavg() have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference.\n\n- [sysconf()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/s/sysconf.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/s/sysconf.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "system",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX013",
              "name": "ProcPathsLinuxLayout",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "/proc paths (Linux layout)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "QNX has a /proc, but it is a process-manager namespace with its own layout, not Linux text files such as /proc/cpuinfo, /proc/meminfo, /proc/stat or /proc/<pid>/stat."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Use the QNX /proc interface (devctl() with DCMD_PROC_* commands) described in the Programmer's Guide, sysconf(), or pidin-style queries instead of parsing Linux files. For the executable path, QNX provides /proc/self/exefile (verify on your target). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Use the QNX /proc interface (devctl() with DCMD_PROC_* commands) described in the Programmer's Guide, sysconf(), or pidin-style queries instead of parsing Linux files. For the executable path, QNX provides /proc/self/exefile (verify on your target).\n\n**Why:** QNX has a /proc, but it is a process-manager namespace with its own layout, not Linux text files such as /proc/cpuinfo, /proc/meminfo, /proc/stat or /proc/<pid>/stat.\n\n- [Controlling processes via the /proc filesystem (Programmer's Guide)](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.prog/topic/process_proc_filesystem.html)\n- [devctl()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/d/devctl.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.prog/topic/process_proc_filesystem.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "system",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX014",
              "name": "SysSysfsPaths",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "/sys (sysfs) paths"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "QNX does not provide Linux sysfs. Paths such as /sys/class/gpio, /sys/class/net or /sys/devices have no QNX counterpart."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Use the QNX driver/resource-manager interface for the device (GPIO, I2C, SPI, networking APIs, ...). Which one depends on your BSP: verify in the BSP documentation. (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Use the QNX driver/resource-manager interface for the device (GPIO, I2C, SPI, networking APIs, ...). Which one depends on your BSP: verify in the BSP documentation.\n\n**Why:** QNX does not provide Linux sysfs. Paths such as /sys/class/gpio, /sys/class/net or /sys/devices have no QNX counterpart.\n\n- [devctl()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/d/devctl.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/d/devctl.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "system",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX018",
              "name": "LinuxSpecificIoctlRequests",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Linux-specific ioctl requests"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ioctl request codes such as BLK*, FS_IOC_*, FICLONE, SIOCETHTOOL, EVIOC*, I2C_*/SPI_IOC_* and TUNSETIFF are Linux definitions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: QNX resource managers are usually controlled with devctl() and DCMD_* commands. Map each request to the corresponding resource-manager command for your driver (verify per driver). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** QNX resource managers are usually controlled with devctl() and DCMD_* commands. Map each request to the corresponding resource-manager command for your driver (verify per driver).\n\n**Why:** ioctl request codes such as BLK*, FS_IOC_*, FICLONE, SIOCETHTOOL, EVIOC*, I2C_*/SPI_IOC_* and TUNSETIFF are Linux definitions.\n\n- [devctl()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/d/devctl.html)\n- [ioctl(), vioctl()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/i/ioctl.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/d/devctl.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "io",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX019",
              "name": "LinuxGlibcPreprocessorGuards",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Linux/glibc preprocessor guards"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Code under #ifdef __linux__ / __GLIBC__ is not compiled by qcc for QNX, so such branches silently disappear and some #else branches may pick a BSD or generic path that was never tested on QNX."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Add an explicit __QNXNTO__ (and/or __QNX__) branch, or restructure so the portable code path is the default. qcc predefines the QNX macros: confirm with 'qcc -V... -dM -E - < /dev/null' on your SDP (verify). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Add an explicit __QNXNTO__ (and/or __QNX__) branch, or restructure so the portable code path is the default. qcc predefines the QNX macros: confirm with 'qcc -V... -dM -E - < /dev/null' on your SDP (verify).\n\n**Why:** Code under #ifdef __linux__ / __GLIBC__ is not compiled by qcc for QNX, so such branches silently disappear and some #else branches may pick a BSD or generic path that was never tested on QNX.\n\n- [qcc / q++ (Utilities Reference)](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.utilities/topic/q/qcc.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.utilities/topic/q/qcc.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "portability",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX020",
              "name": "GlibcGnuExtensions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "glibc / GNU extensions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "These GNU extensions have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference (they may still be available in your SDP headers or not: the compiler is the judge)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Provide a small portable replacement in your own code (strchrnul/memrchr/qsort_r/secure_getenv/canonicalize_file_name/get_current_dir_name are a few lines each), or guard the use. Do not assume the glibc behaviour (verify). (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Provide a small portable replacement in your own code (strchrnul/memrchr/qsort_r/secure_getenv/canonicalize_file_name/get_current_dir_name are a few lines each), or guard the use. Do not assume the glibc behaviour (verify).\n\n**Why:** These GNU extensions have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference (they may still be available in your SDP headers or not: the compiler is the judge).\n\n- [realpath()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/r/realpath.html)\n- [getopt_long()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/g/getopt_long.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/r/realpath.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "portability",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX022",
              "name": "RawSyscallSys_*Numbers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "raw syscall() / SYS_* numbers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Linux system-call numbers do not exist on QNX; syscall() has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Call the libc/QNX API instead. syscall(SYS_gettid) -> gettid() (documented, <process.h>). Others: find the libc function, or QNX kernel call, that provides the feature. (confidence: documented)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Call the libc/QNX API instead. syscall(SYS_gettid) -> gettid() (documented, <process.h>). Others: find the libc function, or QNX kernel call, that provides the feature.\n\n**Why:** Linux system-call numbers do not exist on QNX; syscall() has no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference.\n\n- [gettid()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/g/gettid.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/g/gettid.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "process",
                  "confidence-documented"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "documented"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX023",
              "name": "Accept4Dup3Sock_*CreationFlags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "accept4 / dup3 / SOCK_* creation flags"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "accept4 and dup3 have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference; SOCK_NONBLOCK/SOCK_CLOEXEC support depends on the network stack headers of your SDP."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "QNX 8.0 strategy: Use accept()/dup2()/socket() followed by fcntl(fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC) and fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK) (a small wrapper keeps the call sites clean). Note the non-atomic window if you fork concurrently (verify). pipe2() and mkostemp() are documented and can stay. (confidence: verify)",
                "markdown": "**QNX 8.0 strategy:** Use accept()/dup2()/socket() followed by fcntl(fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC) and fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK) (a small wrapper keeps the call sites clean). Note the non-atomic window if you fork concurrently (verify). pipe2() and mkostemp() are documented and can stay.\n\n**Why:** accept4 and dup3 have no page in the QNX 8.0 C Library Reference; SOCK_NONBLOCK/SOCK_CLOEXEC support depends on the network stack headers of your SDP.\n\n- [pipe(), pipe2()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/pipe.html)\n- [mkostemp()](https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/m/mkostemp.html)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://www.qnx.com/developers/docs/8.0/com.qnx.doc.neutrino.lib_ref/topic/p/pipe.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "qnx",
                  "qnx-8.0",
                  "porting",
                  "io",
                  "confidence-verify"
                ],
                "qnxport-confidence": "verify"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "QNX025",
              "name": "StatfsSysVfs.H",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "statfs / sys/vfs.h"
              },
